  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1011 AM MDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nMoisture levels begin to increase today as the subtropical high \nstretches eastward from the Four Corners. The best moisture will \nremain out west today with PWs near 1.2\" in the NM Bootheel and a\nSlight ERO from WPC. HREF/REFS show rainfall potential of about \n2\" in Hidalgo Co into the overnight with isolated flash flooding.\nStorm motion will be slow to the SW this afternoon and evening \nwith gusty outflows over 40 mph. PWs rise to about 1\" for most of \nthe lowlands, enough to spark spotty showers or storms this \nafternoon. Rain chances will be around 10% E of Deming today (NBM \nwas bumped up to reflect HRRR/CAMs), higher for the Sacs.\n\nAs large-scale troughing develops out west, the upper high \nconcentrates over the Gulf Coast starting Wed. Our monsoonal flow \nwill strengthen from the S-SE as a result for the rest of the week \nwith PWs climbing to the 1.3-1.4\" range for KELP into the weekend\naccording to the LREF, which is near the 90th %-ile for mid-\nAugust. Shortwaves/vort maxes are sure to pass overhead or near \nthe region from Wed-Sun to give us extra lift, but model \nuncertainty will prevent us from knowing specific impacts until \nthose features materialize. Global ensembles indicate a system \ncoming up the Rio Grande from the Gulf this weekend with Sat \nappearing to be the most active day at the moment. Until Sat,\nisolated to scattered storms are forecast each day, favoring\nwestern areas for a higher flood threat.\n\nLate in the period, the high looks to elongate and drift westward \nthrough the Southern Plains, allowing moisture levels and storm \nchances to decrease early next week. Temperatures remain warm today \nwith lowland highs around 100F. We'll cool off closer to normal \nafter today as moisture/rain chances increase and the upper high \nwanders to the east.",
